# Rust Axum Backend Standards

> Apply these standards when working with backend Rust files.

## Rust Backend Architecture

The backend uses Rust with Axum framework for maximum type safety and compile-time guarantees.

## Project Structure

```
backend/
├── src/
│   ├── main.rs           # Application entry point
│   ├── lib.rs            # Library exports
│   ├── api/              # API endpoint handlers
│   │   ├── mod.rs        # Router configuration
│   │   ├── auth.rs       # Authentication endpoints
│   │   ├── llm.rs        # LLM endpoints
│   │   └── vectordb.rs   # Vector database endpoints
│   ├── config/           # Configuration management
│   │   └── mod.rs        # Settings from environment
│   ├── models/           # Data models
│   │   ├── mod.rs        # Module exports
│   │   ├── auth.rs       # Auth-related types
│   │   ├── error.rs      # Error types
│   │   ├── llm.rs        # LLM types
│   │   └── vectordb.rs   # Vector DB types
│   └── services/         # Business logic
│       ├── mod.rs        # Module exports
│       ├── llm/          # LLM services
│       ├── supabase/     # Supabase services
│       └── vectordb/     # Vector DB services
├── Cargo.toml            # Dependencies
├── Dockerfile            # Production image
└── Dockerfile.dev        # Development image
```

## Endpoint Pattern

Always use the `#[axum::debug_handler]` attribute for better error messages:

```rust
use axum::{extract::Json, http::HeaderMap, routing::post, Router};
use crate::models::{AppError, CreateItemRequest, ItemResponse};

pub fn router() -> Router {
    Router::new()
        .route("/items", post(create_item))
}

/// Create a new item.
#[axum::debug_handler]
async fn create_item(
    headers: HeaderMap,
    Json(request): Json<CreateItemRequest>,
) -> Result<Json<ItemResponse>, AppError> {
    // 1. Authenticate
    let user = authenticate(&headers).await?;
    
    // 2. Validate
    use validator::Validate;
    request.validate().map_err(AppError::from)?;
    
    // 3. Execute business logic
    let service = MyService::new()?;
    let result = service.create_item(&request).await?;
    
    // 4. Return response
    Ok(Json(result))
}
```

## Error Handling

Use the `AppError` enum for all errors:

```rust
use thiserror::Error;
use axum::http::StatusCode;

#[derive(Debug, Error)]
pub enum AppError {
    #[error("Authentication failed: {0}")]
    Unauthorized(String),
    
    #[error("Invalid request: {0}")]
    BadRequest(String),
    
    #[error("Not found: {0}")]
    NotFound(String),
    
    #[error("External service error: {0}")]
    ExternalService(String),
    
    #[error("Internal error: {0}")]
    Internal(String),
}

impl AppError {
    pub const fn status_code(&self) -> StatusCode {
        match self {
            Self::Unauthorized(_) => StatusCode::UNAUTHORIZED,
            Self::BadRequest(_) => StatusCode::BAD_REQUEST,
            Self::NotFound(_) => StatusCode::NOT_FOUND,
            Self::ExternalService(_) => StatusCode::BAD_GATEWAY,
            Self::Internal(_) => StatusCode::INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR,
        }
    }
}

impl IntoResponse for AppError {
    fn into_response(self) -> Response {
        let status = self.status_code();
        let body = Json(json!({ "error": self.to_string() }));
        (status, body).into_response()
    }
}
```

## Model Definitions

Use serde for serialization and validator for validation:

```rust
use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};
use validator::Validate;

/// Request to create an item.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Deserialize, Validate)]
pub struct CreateItemRequest {
    /// Item name
    #[validate(length(min = 1, max = 100))]
    pub name: String,
    
    /// Optional description
    #[serde(skip_serializing_if = "Option::is_none")]
    pub description: Option<String>,
    
    /// Item count (must be positive)
    #[serde(default = "default_count")]
    #[validate(range(min = 1, max = 1000))]
    pub count: u32,
}

fn default_count() -> u32 { 1 }

/// Response for item operations.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct ItemResponse {
    pub id: String,
    pub name: String,
    pub description: Option<String>,
    pub count: u32,
    pub created_at: chrono::DateTime<chrono::Utc>,
}
```

## Service Layer

Use traits for abstraction and testability:

```rust
use async_trait::async_trait;
use crate::models::AppError;

/// Trait for item services.
#[async_trait]
pub trait ItemService: Send + Sync {
    async fn create(&self, request: &CreateItemRequest) -> Result<ItemResponse, AppError>;
    async fn get(&self, id: &str) -> Result<Option<ItemResponse>, AppError>;
    async fn list(&self) -> Result<Vec<ItemResponse>, AppError>;
    async fn delete(&self, id: &str) -> Result<bool, AppError>;
}

/// Concrete implementation.
pub struct SupabaseItemService {
    client: Client,
}

#[async_trait]
impl ItemService for SupabaseItemService {
    async fn create(&self, request: &CreateItemRequest) -> Result<ItemResponse, AppError> {
        // Implementation
    }
    // ... other methods
}
```

## Configuration

Use strong typing for configuration:

```rust
use once_cell::sync::Lazy;

pub static SETTINGS: Lazy<Settings> = Lazy::new(|| {
    Settings::from_env().expect("Failed to load settings")
});

#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
pub struct Settings {
    pub environment: Environment,
    pub server: ServerConfig,
    pub supabase: SupabaseConfig,
    pub llm: LlmConfig,
}

impl Settings {
    pub fn from_env() -> Result<Self, SettingsError> {
        let _ = dotenvy::dotenv(); // Load .env if present
        
        Ok(Self {
            environment: env::var("ENVIRONMENT")
                .unwrap_or_else(|_| "development".to_string())
                .parse()?,
            // ... other fields
        })
    }
}
```

## Logging

Use tracing for structured logging:

```rust
use tracing::{info, error, warn, debug};

async fn process_request(user_id: &str, request: &Request) -> Result<Response, AppError> {
    info!(user_id = %user_id, "Processing request");
    
    let result = service.process(request).await.map_err(|e| {
        error!(user_id = %user_id, error = %e, "Request processing failed");
        AppError::Internal(e.to_string())
    })?;
    
    debug!(user_id = %user_id, result_id = %result.id, "Request completed");
    
    Ok(result)
}
```

## Testing

Write tests using Rust's built-in test framework:

```rust
#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;
    use axum::{body::Body, http::{Request, StatusCode}};
    use tower::ServiceExt;

    #[tokio::test]
    async fn test_create_item() {
        let app = create_app();
        
        let response = app
            .oneshot(
                Request::builder()
                    .method("POST")
                    .uri("/api/items")
                    .header("Content-Type", "application/json")
                    .header("Authorization", "Bearer test_token")
                    .body(Body::from(r#"{"name":"test"}"#))
                    .unwrap()
            )
            .await
            .unwrap();
        
        assert_eq!(response.status(), StatusCode::OK);
    }
}
```

## Clippy Lints

The project enforces strict linting:

```toml
[lints.rust]
unsafe_code = "forbid"

[lints.clippy]
all = "warn"
pedantic = "warn"
nursery = "warn"
unwrap_used = "warn"
expect_used = "warn"
```

**Key rules:**
- No `unsafe` code
- No `.unwrap()` or `.expect()` - use proper error handling
- All clippy warnings treated as errors in CI

